    "reason": "Train and evaluation caches use the same frozen repository commit, checkpoint, image preprocessing, 24-to-16 resampling, token normalization, shape, order, and float16 storage contract, but they were produced under different Torch and ROCm versions. A small numerical or kernel-domain shift could hurt cross-role readout generalization, so the negative result should be scoped to the executed bound runtimes rather than treated as a runtime-invariant impossibility result.",
    "positive_gate_credibility": "The persistence, wrong-scene, current-state, and action-mean controls share the same evaluation cache and train-to-evaluation runtime boundary. The strict persistence superiority and exact replay therefore remain credible within the executed interface, and the random comparison is unaffected as a fixed expectation.",
    "why_failed_gates_are_not_rescued": "No matched-runtime counterfactual was executed, so the runtime difference supplies no measured sign or magnitude and cannot be used post hoc to reverse a preregistered gate. The retained physical baseline is significantly better with the entire registered interval above zero, task/action has a better point regret, and several controls that share the V-JEPA runtime boundary also fail. The caveat narrows generalization of the STOP; it does not qualify the interface or authorize another attempt."
